    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the problem.\n\nStep 1 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nclass EditorHistory<T> {\n  private readonly undoStack: T[] = [];\n  private readonly redoStack: T[] = [];\n  private current: T;\n\n  constructor(initial: T) {\n    this.current = initial;\n  }\n\n  apply(next: T): void {\n    this.undoStack.push(this.current);\n    this.current = next;\n    this.redoStack.length = 0;\n  }\n\n  undo(): T {\n    const previous = this.undoStack.pop();\n    if (previous === undefined) return this.current;\n\n    this.redoStack.push(this.current);\n    this.current = previous;\n    return this.current;\n  }\n\n  redo(): T {\n    const next = this.redoStack.pop();\n    if (next === undefined) return this.current;\n\n    this.undoStack.push(this.current);\n    this.current = next;\n    return this.current;\n  }\n\n  value(): T {\n    return this.current;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — WITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction undoRedoUsingArrays<T>(\n  history: readonly T[],\n  index: number,\n  direction: \"undo\" | \"redo\",\n): { value: T; index: number } {\n  const nextIndex =\n    direction === \"undo\"\n      ? Math.max(0, index - 1)\n      : Math.min(history.length - 1, index + 1);\n\n  return {\n    value: history[nextIndex]!,\n    index: nextIndex,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nStep 3 — Example:\nA → B → C\nundo → B\nundo → A\nredo → B\napply D after undo → redo history is cleared.\n\nStep 4 — Complexity:\nStack operations are O(1).\nMemory O(k) for stored history, where k is the number of retained states/actions.\n\nStep 5 — Interview takeaway:\nUse the simplest implementation that preserves correctness and keeps UI work bounded.",
