    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the problem.\n\nStep 1 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE:\n```ts\nclass BrowserHistory {\n  private readonly entries: string[] = [];\n  private current = -1;\n\n  visit(url: string): void {\n    this.entries.splice(this.current + 1);\n    this.entries.push(url);\n    this.current = this.entries.length - 1;\n  }\n\n  back(): string | null {\n    if (this.current <= 0) return this.entries[0] ?? null;\n    this.current--;\n    return this.entries[this.current]!;\n  }\n\n  forward(): string | null {\n    if (this.current >= this.entries.length - 1) {\n      return this.entries[this.current] ?? null;\n    }\n    this.current++;\n    return this.entries[this.current]!;\n  }\n\n  currentUrl(): string | null {\n    return this.entries[this.current] ?? null;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — WITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction truncateForwardHistory(history: readonly string[], current: number): string[] {\n  return history.slice(0, current + 1);\n}\n```\n\nStep 3 — Example:\nA → B → C\nback → B\nvisit D → A, B, D\nforward is no longer C.\n\nStep 4 — Complexity:\nWith Array.splice, truncation can be O(n) in the amount removed.\nBack/forward cursor movement is O(1).\n\nStep 5 — Interview takeaway:\nUse the simplest implementation that preserves correctness and keeps UI work bounded.",
